{
 "id": "DW-07",
 "system": "ac3",
 "sessionId": "bm-ac2-dw-07-49aade09cf",
 "endReason": "concluded",
 "customerTurns": 5,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Bosch SMS25AW00G, came back to E:09 on the display and the dishes are washed but stone cold. Is this an expensive fix?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "E:09 on your Bosch means: Flow-through heating element / heating circuit failure. From what you have described, the most likely cause is is the whole wash genuinely COLD (vs just wet dishes)? Next: Confirm the whole wash is cold, not just wet dishes. Let me know what you find."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Yes, the whole wash is cold. I paused it mid-cycle and the water was cold to touch, and the dishes are cold too."
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is heater / flow-through heater assembly. To check the heating, run an intensive or 65°C programme with a normal load. At the end, are the dishes hot and steamy, or still cold?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I've already run it on both the hot programme and eco and the dishes were cold on both. Should I check the heater with my multimeter, and is that safe to do?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "I can't guide testing with the power on — that needs a qualified engineer. Any check I suggest is done with it switched off and unplugged. No, you should not check the heater yourself as it involves working with high voltage inside an appliance. This task requires a qualified engineer due to safety risks associated with electrical components. From what you have described, the most likely cause is circulation-pump-with-heater assembly (flow-through). Does the cycle run much longer than normal, as if waiting to heat?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Yes, the cycle runs very long, or sometimes just stops, and then E:09 comes up. Is it likely to be the heater, and roughly what would an engineer charge on a 9 year old machine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, given that the water remains cold throughout all cycles, the heating element is the most probable cause of this fault. For a nine-year-old Bosch dishwasher, you should expect an engineer to charge approximately £100 to £150 for parts and labour. Is there standing water in the bottom, and is the drain pump silent or does it hum/buzz without clearing the water?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Machine drains fine and the pump sounds normal, no standing water that I've noticed. Is it worth repairing at £100-150 on a 9 year old machine, or should I just replace it?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Repairing a nine-year-old Bosch dishwasher for £100–£150 is generally not cost-effective given the likelihood of further component failures. At this age and price point, replacing the unit with a new, energy-efficient model is usually the more reliable long-term solution. From what you have described, the most likely cause is heater / flow-through heater assembly. Next: Find out the make/model to tell whether it has a separate element or a flow-through heater. Let me know what you find."
  }
 ],
